            "body": "Some measurements in support of this fix, from a beam-search benchmark on a fork whose `_wait_and_warmup` matches main's: Qwen3-0.6B, 2,048-token prompts, 256 beams, `--skip-server-warmup`, one RTX PRO 6000 with an AMD EPYC 9124 host.\n\n- Without the freeze, the startup heap stays in GC scope: about 1.0M tracked objects in the tokenizer manager and 1.1M in the scheduler. Each full collection took 0.39 s and 0.48 s, with the process stalled.\n- The same server with only the freeze fixed, one round of 600 requests per concurrency, GC logging on:\n\n  | Concurrency | Freeze refused: mean / p99 / max (ms) | Freeze working: mean / p99 / max (ms) |\n  | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n  | 2 | 54.2 / 109 / 520 | 52.9 / 87 / 124 |\n  | 8 | 169.2 / 630 / 635 | 153.9 / 288 / 300 |\n\nOne suggestion: report `ServerStatus.Up` only after the freeze. With Up first, clients can start sending requests while the freeze is still pending. In our runs a version with that order had the freeze land on the server's first few requests, adding 40-70 ms to each. Our version waits for the listener with the same `/model_info` poll that the warmup path already uses, then freezes, then sets Up: https://github.com/lauthu/sglang/commit/3ab9a26278a706a7347d4b4ff3a41f69bf4b02a4 (with a test that starts the listener late). Happy to adapt it to this PR, or to leave it as a reference.\n\n#37063 proposed the same retry and was closed by the idle-PR bot, not on review.\n",
